1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Orbit Lab: why doesn't every new moon cause an eclipse?
A spatial lesson for curious learners.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:16,000
Live Lens Studio Preview • simulated pinch input
Start reveals a world-space Sun, Earth and Moon model.

3
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:24,000
Check the wrong alignment: feedback helps you recover.
The lesson advances only when the arrangement is correct.

4
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:37,000
Move the Moon with the slider or precise ±15° steps.
Sizes and distances are exaggerated teaching aids.

5
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:47,000
Solar eclipse: Moon between Sun and Earth.
Its violet shadow outline reaches Earth.

6
00:00:47,000 --> 00:01:08,000
Now find the opposite alignment for a lunar eclipse.
The learner manipulates one shared 3D model.

7
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:25,000
Lunar eclipse: Earth between Sun and Moon.
The Moon enters Earth's shadow.

8
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:36,000
Same order, different plane: the tilted shadow misses.
The 18° teaching tilt exaggerates the real effect.

9
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:47,000
Flatten the orbit and check again.
Real eclipses occur near orbital nodes.

10
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:54,000
Three challenges complete. Replay restores the lesson.
Order and orbital plane both matter.

11
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:01,200
Built with Codex, CLAD and Lens Studio MCP; five LEAF tests passed.
Offline lesson. Real-device comfort and tracking remain untested.
